		</section>
		<!-- LEDGER -->
		<section class="band" id="ledger">
			<div class="wrap">
				<header class="band-head">
					<span class="eyebrow">Where the saving comes from</span>
					<h2 class="t-section">The second read is free</h2>
					<p class="lede">
						A filter removes a line because a pattern calls it noise. The ledger removes it because your agent is already holding it. Filters are what every tool in this category ships. The ledger is the part that is not.
					</p>
				</header>

				<dl class="rows">
					{ledger.map((r) => (
						<div class={`row${r.quiet ? ' row-quiet' : ''}`}>
							<dt class={`row-k${r.sub ? ' is-mono' : ''}`}>{r.k}{r.sub && <span class="sub">{r.sub}</span>}</dt>
							<dd class="row-v" set:html={r.v} />
						</div>
					))}
				</dl>

				<p class="fine" style="margin-top:18px;max-width:660px">
					One marker carries all of it: <code>[OMNI: 40 lines already shown, omni retrieve bc7e821a4340073e]</code>. The count, what happened to those lines, and the handle that undoes it.
				</p>

				{/* The only numbers on the page not parsed at build: readme-stats.js
				    reads the class table, not the head-to-head one. They come from the
				    head-to-head in docs/website/src/develop/benchmarks.md, replayed on
				    0.7.5, and the link is here so a reader can check every row.

				    Re-read that table before touching them, and re-read it whenever the
				    corpus changes. The previous version of this sentence said our
				    filters take 2.7% against rtk's 6.2% and conceded the row. Those were
				    the 0.7.4 corpus's figures left behind on a page whose trace count is
				    parsed, so once the parser was fixed the sentence would have
				    attributed one corpus's result to another corpus's count. */}
				<p class="fine" style="margin-top:14px;max-width:660px">
					Head to head on the same {stats.totalCalls} traces, identical bytes into every arm, OMNI with its ledger is the top row at 69.6%. The closest arm is headroom's dedup at 65.8%, then lean-ctx at 49.4%; rtk's filters take 6.2% and reach 61.4% once our ledger is bolted on, which is the clearest statement of where the work happens. Our filters alone take 32.6%, and lean-ctx beats that sub-component on a corpus built for exactly that shape. Every row is published, that one included.
					{' '}
					<span class="receipt"><a href="/docs/develop/benchmarks">benchmarks</a></span>
				</p>
			</div>
		</section>
